/// United States individual retirement accounts: traditional and Roth.
///
/// Both are tax-deferred and share a yearly contribution limit and the 10%
/// tax on early withdrawals. They differ in when tax is paid. A deducted
/// traditional contribution is untaxed, so everything that comes out is income.
/// A Roth contribution was taxed already, so only its earnings can ever be.
///
/// The kind says what money arriving in the account is: a traditional IRA takes
/// it with no basis (`basis zero`), and deducts it from income when it came
/// from an account you own, because it was deducted; a Roth takes it at cost. A
/// traditional contribution that was not deducted (Form 8606) is written with
/// its own basis, `basis 6_000 USD`, and marked `#nondeductible`.
///
/// A contribution for last year, made before the tax filing deadline, says
/// `for 2025` and counts toward that year's limit.
///
/// The figures are community-maintained data. Verify them against the IRS
/// (irs.gov, the yearly "COLA" notice) before relying on them, and correct
/// them in a pull request when they are wrong.
system us/ira

use us
use std


/// An individual retirement account of either kind (IRC §408, §408A).
kind ira : tax-deferred
  liquidity 3d

  /// Contributions to IRAs are capped for each person and calendar year, with
  /// a catch-up from age 50 (IRC §219(b)(5)). This deposit took the year over
  /// the cap. The limit is shared between a person's traditional and Roth IRAs,
  /// which is why it is counted per person rather than per account. Excess
  /// contributions are charged 6% for every year they stay (IRC §4973).
  ///
  /// To fix: withdraw the excess and its earnings before the tax filing
  /// deadline.
  law contribution-limit
    on in
    when not purpose is #income
    when not from is tax-deferred
    count amount as ira-contributions
    let extra = if owner.age >= 50y then catch-up[year] else empty
    require value(tally(ira-contributions), USD) <= limit[year] + extra "IRA contributions over the yearly limit"

  /// Money taken out before age 59½ carries an additional 10% tax on the part
  /// that is taxable (IRC §72(t)). This withdrawal is priced at that 10%.
  ///
  /// To fix: if an exception applies (disability, a first home up to 10,000
  /// USD, higher education costs, and others in §72(t)(2)), keep the withdrawal
  /// and mark it `!` with the reason.
  law early-withdrawal
    on gain
    when gain > empty
    when not to is market | tax-deferred
    require owner.age >= 59y6m else owe 10% * gain to irs by date(year + 1, 4, 15) as early-withdrawal-penalty "IRA withdrawal before age 59½"

/// A traditional IRA (IRC §408): what comes out is ordinary income, except the
/// part that is basis, what you put in without a deduction.
kind traditional-ira : ira
  basis zero
  deductible true
  select prorata

  /// From age 73, an owner must take a minimum distribution from a traditional
  /// IRA every year (IRC §408(a)(6), §401(a)(9)). It is the balance at the end
  /// of the previous year divided by a divisor from the IRS Uniform Lifetime
  /// Table (Pub. 590-B), 26.5 at age 73. Nothing came out this year. The first
  /// one may wait until April 1 of the following year, and owners born in 1960
  /// or later start at 75.
  ///
  /// To fix: take the distribution before December 31, or the shortfall is
  /// taxed at 25% (IRC §4974).
  law required-minimum-distribution
    each year
    when owner.age >= 73y
    when self.balance > empty
    warn total(out, year) > empty "no required minimum distribution taken this year"

/// A Roth IRA (IRC §408A): contributions are taxed already, so only earnings
/// can be taxed. Set `established` to the day of the first contribution.
kind roth-ira : ira
  basis cost
  select hifo
  taxed-by-kind true
  has established date

  /// A Roth withdrawal is tax-free when it is qualified (IRC §408A(d)(2)): the
  /// owner is 59½ or older and the first contribution is at least five years
  /// old. Contributions come out first and always tax-free (they are the basis,
  /// which is why this account relieves its highest-basis money first), so only
  /// the earnings in a withdrawal are tested. These earnings were not
  /// qualified, so they count as income. The five years are counted from the
  /// day of the first contribution, a little stricter than the IRS, which counts
  /// from January 1 of that year.
  ///
  /// To fix: `established` must be the day of the first contribution to any Roth
  /// IRA. If a withdrawal is qualified for another reason (a first home,
  /// disability), keep it and count nothing.
  law count-taxable-earnings
    on gain
    when gain > empty
    when not to is market | tax-deferred
    let qualified = owner.age >= 59y6m and date >= self.established + 5y
    when not qualified
    count gain as distributions
    count gain as total-income


// The contribution limit for traditional and Roth IRAs combined (IRC §219(b)(5)).
// Notice 2023-75 (2024), Notice 2024-80 (2025), Notice 2025-67 (2026).
// verify: the 2026 figures against Notice 2025-67.
param limit
  2024 7_000 USD
  2025 7_000 USD
  2026 7_500 USD

// The catch-up for age 50 and over (IRC §219(b)(5)(B)), indexed since 2024.
param catch-up
  2024 1_000 USD
  2025 1_000 USD
  2026 1_100 USD
